Subject: Place the rendered preview canvas with a transform, not a snapped box

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-100). The rendered preview canvas was laid out as
a left/top/width/height box around the player. Browsers snap such a box to
whole device pixels, so whenever the edge padding misses a device-pixel
boundary (most fit scales) the whole picture moved and rescaled by a fraction
of a pixel against the DOM player, whose content is placed by a transform.
An item edge then differed between the two presentations in the display
column it falls in. The canvas is now laid out at its backing size and placed
by the same kind of transform, so both presentations share one mapping.
Backing size, padding and the central copy are unchanged.

Measured in Chromium at device pixel ratio 1: both presentations are then
identical at every tested edge position. This patch does not change the
preview render size, so on a high-density display the canvas is still
magnified from project resolution and its edges are filtered differently
from the DOM player's.

diff --git a/src/features/preview/utils/preview-display-canvas.ts b/src/features/preview/utils/preview-display-canvas.ts
--- a/src/features/preview/utils/preview-display-canvas.ts
+++ b/src/features/preview/utils/preview-display-canvas.ts
@@ -80,16 +80,29 @@
   top: string
   width: string
   height: string
+  maxWidth: string
+  maxHeight: string
+  transformOrigin: string
+  transform: string
 } {
   const padding = getPreviewDisplayEdgePadding(playerSize, renderSize)
-  const padX = renderSize.width > 0 ? (playerSize.width / renderSize.width) * padding : padding
-  const padY = renderSize.height > 0 ? (playerSize.height / renderSize.height) * padding : padding
-
+  const backingSize = getPreviewDisplayCanvasBackingSize(playerSize, renderSize)
+  const scaleX = renderSize.width > 0 ? playerSize.width / renderSize.width : 1
+  const scaleY = renderSize.height > 0 ? playerSize.height / renderSize.height : 1
+
+  // Lay the canvas out at its backing size and place it with a transform, the
+  // way the DOM player places its content. A left/width box is snapped to whole
+  // device pixels, so padding that misses a device-pixel boundary moved and
+  // rescaled the whole picture by a fraction of a pixel against the player.
   return {
-    left: `-${padX}px`,
-    top: `-${padY}px`,
-    width: `calc(100% + ${padX * 2}px)`,
-    height: `calc(100% + ${padY * 2}px)`,
+    left: '0px',
+    top: '0px',
+    width: `${backingSize.width}px`,
+    height: `${backingSize.height}px`,
+    maxWidth: 'none',
+    maxHeight: 'none',
+    transformOrigin: '0 0',
+    transform: `translate(${-padding * scaleX}px, ${-padding * scaleY}px) scale(${scaleX}, ${scaleY})`,
   }
 }
 
